Two instances of Spring Boot app running as separate containers, both connecting to a single Postgres container

1. Docker Compose File (docker-compose.yml)

version: '3.9'

services:
  db:
    image: postgres:15
    container_name: my_postgres
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: myuser
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: mypassword
      POSTGRES_DB: mydb
    ports:
      - "5432:5432"
    volumes:
      - db_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

  app1:
    image: my-springboot-app:latest
    container_name: spring_app1
    environment:
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/mydb
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME: myuser
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD: mypassword
    depends_on:
      - db
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"

  app2:
    image: my-springboot-app:latest
    container_name: spring_app2
    environment:
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/mydb
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME: myuser
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD: mypassword
    depends_on:
      - db
    ports:
      - "8081:8080"

volumes:
  db_data:

  
2️. Explanation
	- db service:
		-- Runs PostgreSQL.
		-- Exposes 5432 to host (optional, mainly for local access).
		-- Uses a named volume db_data for persistence.
	- app1 and app2 services:
		-- Both run your Spring Boot image.
		-- SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL points to db:5432 — Docker Compose creates an internal network where service names act as hostnames.
		-- app1 exposes 8080 to host, app2 exposes 8081.
	- depends_on ensures the DB container starts before the apps.

3️. How to Start
	- docker-compose up -d
	- This will start 3 containers:
		-- my_postgres
		-- spring_app1
		-- spring_app2
		-- Both apps will connect to the same Postgres DB automatically.
		
This setup is production-friendly for development and testing.		